<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maly Trostenets</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maly Trostenets was a Nazi extermination site near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> in occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, in use from 1942 to 1944. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from Minsk and others deported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, as well as Soviet prisoners and partisans, were shot there, mostly in the Blagovshchina forest, or killed in gas vans. Estimates of the number of dead range from about 60,000 to more than 200,000.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maly-trostenets.jpg" alt="Maly Trostenets" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Epoz8 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maly_Trostinets_Field_of_Burial.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Maly Trostenets was an extermination site and forced-labour camp operated by Nazi Germany in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, twelve kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a>. It comprised two sites: a forced-labour camp, established at an uncertain date between November 1941 and January 1942, and a nearby site in the Blagovshchina Forest intended solely for mass murder. In April and May 1942 Eduard Strauch, the commander of the Security Police and SD in Minsk, took over the 250-hectare <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> collective farm for the camp. Soviet, Polish and Western and Central European Jews who had been held in the Minsk Ghetto were imprisoned there, along with local suspected partisans, and the number of inmates mostly stood between 500 and 1,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In March and April 1942 Adolf Eichmann, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-himmler">Heinrich Himmler</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinhard-heydrich">Reinhard Heydrich</a> came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> and decided to resume <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> there and to have deportees murdered on arrival. From May to October 1942 transports came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Most deportees were shot soon after arrival and buried in mass graves; gas vans were in use by June 1942 at the latest.</p><p>By August 1942 a railway siding had been extended so that trains could go directly to the killing site. The largest massacre, on 28 to 30 July 1942, killed about 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of the Minsk Ghetto who were judged unable to work.</p><p>From October 1942 central European Jews were sent instead to the extermination camps in occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. After the Minsk Ghetto was liquidated in October 1943, prisoners were forced to exhume and burn the bodies in Sonderaktion 1005, and were then murdered. From late 1943 the Shashkovka Forest replaced Blagovshchina as the place where local non-Jewish civilians, suspected partisans and prisoners were killed. On 29 and 30 June 1944, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> advanced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-bagration">Operation Bagration</a>, German forces killed the remaining prisoners in a barn and burned the camp.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The death toll is hard to establish because the graves were opened and the bodies burned from October 1943. Soviet postwar estimates, among them Ilya Ehrenburg's figure of 206,500, counted all victims of the surrounding execution sites, and the figure stood on a memorial sign in 2015 without mention of Jewish victims.</p><p>Recent research gives lower numbers: Christian Gerlach and Petra Rentrop put the toll at about 60,000, while Stephan Lehnstaedt estimated at least 106,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. Scholars regard it as the fourth-largest Nazi extermination site. The killings were not mentioned at the Nuremberg Trials and were largely ignored by Soviet and Belarusian officials until the late 1990s. A memorial complex now stands at the site, and Waltraud Barton collected the names of 10,000 Austrian Jews murdered there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maly Trostenets concentration camp” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Holocaust was carried out on a vast scale: Jews were murdered at Rumbula near Riga, Ponary near Vilnius, the Ninth Fort in Kaunas and <b>Maly Trostenets</b> near Minsk.</blockquote><a href="/state/reichskommissariat-ostland">Reichskommissariat Ostland</a></li></ul></section>
